          "source_text_sha256": "edcb686547ba5e19260a30ecd52ae87668ff11c2d108368f438e1b3a7e0d89d4",
          "start_ms": null,
          "status": "machine-unreviewed",
          "text": "Summary: Observation of a white object zigzagging between two planes: probable observation of an object that fell from a parachute-dropping plane.",
          "translation_text_sha256": "492aa36ec1f1572d900165fc8227be3e8ab4fb5d82135b2f10aff0646442bb23"
        },
        {
          "end_ms": null,
          "error": null,
          "field": "description",
          "page_id": "page-39e0008880c53bae6f49e3d5",
          "qa_findings": [],
          "review_status": "machine-unreviewed",
          "segment_id": "seg-227af7f03025e190b4e6f7fa",
          "segment_index": 13,
          "source_segment_id": "seg-227af7f03025e190b4e6f7fa",
          "source_text_sha256": "d9c984342336a9a8c040c50ed31116e069aab53cefa323dca3320070fd02b841",
          "start_ms": null,
          "status": "machine-unreviewed",
          "text": "Description: On May 30, 2015, around 2:00 PM, a witness observed parachute-dropping planes in the sky and noticed an object between two of them descending in a zigzag pattern with rapid, jerky movements. The object moved away at a phenomenal speed. The GEIPAN contacted the \"Abeille Parachutisme\" club in Honfleur, who confirmed repeated rotations that day to drop parachutists. A club official stated that \"During these rotations, it happens that objects escape from the dropping planes, and these objects never follow a straight trajectory, but rather arabesques linked to air currents.\" Indeed, an object dropped from an airplane at flight speed is subject to: - gravity, which causes it to fall, - aerodynamic forces that slow it down and make it spin, - the dominant wind, which pushes it in its own direction, - and local air currents, sometimes upward. The sum of these forces could explain the observed trajectory, which is completely chaotic because the object in question is certainly very low-density. The shape described by the witness (flat, rectangular) could be that of a cardboard plate. The GEIPAN classifies this case as B, as a probable observation of an object falling from a parachute-dropping plane.",
          "translation_text_sha256": "9953d45fa21befa9f52649df52c09b54e540ac33671d0594fffe8c6b5ff5ca7a"
        }
      ],
